  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThursday seems to be more of the same story as Wednesday, dry with \ntemperatures in the low 90's and very low (~10%) precipitation \nchances in the far western Panhandles. Friday, the ridge over the \nsoutheast US begins to flatten out thanks to an upper-level trough \npropagating eastward across the central US. This allow for \nsouthwesterly upper-level flow to return to the Panhandles, allowing \nlee-side cyclogenesis to develop a surface low-pressure in eastern \nColorado that would drape a surface moisture boundary across the \nPanhandles. Precipitation chances are still uncertain, but they \nfavor the western and north-central combined Panhandles. \n\nInto the weekend, the upper-level trough continues its eastward \npropagation placing the Panhandles in the exit region. This could be \na pattern shift to keep an eye on that could bring widespread rain \nchances to the Panhandles over the weekend and a drop in \ntemperatures as we head into next week. However, uncertainty in the \npositioning of the trough as it swings through is still high, and \nwill have to be monitored in future forecasts. \n\nFerguson",
